**user**:
> How can I make an R plot_ly figure with two panels that have the same y-axis, but where the x-axis is duplicated on the left and right panels.
**assistant**:
> To create a `plot_ly` figure in R with two panels sharing the same y-axis but having duplicated x-axes on the left and right panels, you can use the `subplot` function. Here’s a step-by-step guide:
> 
> 1. **Create Your Plots**: First, construct the individual plots using `plot_ly`. Ensure that both plots share the same range and layout for the y-axis.
> 
> 2. **Use `subplot`**: Combine the plots with `subplot` and customize the axes.
> 
> Here’s a simple example to illustrate:
> 
> ```r
> library(plotly)
> 
> # Create left plot
> left_plot <- plot_ly(x = ~rnorm(50), y = ~rnorm(50), type = 'scatter', mode = 'markers') %>%
>   layout(xaxis = list(title = "Left X-axis"),
>          yaxis = list(title = "Shared Y-axis"))
> 
> # Create right plot
> right_plot <- plot_ly(x = ~rnorm(50), y = ~rnorm(50), type = 'scatter', mode = 'markers') %>%
>   layout(xaxis = list(title = "Right X-axis"),
>          yaxis = list(title = "", showticklabels = FALSE)) # Hide y-axis labels
> 
> # Combine with subplot
> fig <- subplot(left_plot, right_plot, shareY = TRUE, nrows = 1, margin = 0.05) %>%
>   layout(yaxis = list(title = "Shared Y-axis"))
> 
> # Display the plot
> fig
> ```
> 
> ### Explanation:
> 
> - **Creating Plots**: Each plot (`left_plot` and `right_plot`) is created with `plot_ly`, specifying the data and axis titles.
> - **Hide Y-Axis on Right Plot**: In the `right_plot`, the y-axis labels and title are hidden because we want the y-axes to appear shared.
> - **Combining with `subplot`**: `subplot()` is used to arrange both plots side-by-side (`nrows = 1`) with shared Y-axis (`shareY = TRUE`). The `margin` parameter controls the spacing between the plots.
> - **Final Layout**: Use `layout()` to make any final adjustments, such as setting the y-axis title.
> 
> This setup effectively gives the appearance of a shared y-axis, while independently displaying x-axes for each panel. Adjust data and layout options as needed based on your specific requirements.
